grade(7);
T("Grammar","Clauses and sentence types","Simple, compound and complex","🧩","",
 ["A clause has a subject and a verb. An independent clause can stand alone as a sentence.", "A compound sentence joins two independent clauses with a word like and, but or so.", "A complex sentence has one independent clause and at least one dependent clause, which starts with a word like because, although or when."],
 ["Which sentence is a simple sentence?|The dog barked loudly.|The dog barked, and the cat ran.|When the dog barked, the cat ran.|The dog barked because it was scared.", "Which sentence is a compound sentence?|I was tired, so I went to bed.|Because I was tired, I went to bed.|I went to bed early.|Tired and sleepy, I went to bed.", "Which sentence is a complex sentence?|Although it rained, we played outside.|It rained, but we played outside.|It rained all day.|We played outside and inside.", "Which of these is a dependent clause?|because the bus was late|the bus was late|we waited for the bus|the bus arrived", "Which word is a coordinating conjunction?|but|because|although|when", "Which word is a subordinating conjunction?|although|and|or|so", "What is wrong with this: \"Running down the street.\"|It is a sentence fragment|It is a run-on sentence|It has a spelling mistake|Nothing is wrong", "What is wrong with this: \"I like pizza I eat it every week.\"|It is a run-on sentence|It is a fragment|It needs a question mark|Nothing is wrong", "Which is the best way to fix \"She was late she missed the bus\"?|She was late because she missed the bus.|She was late she, missed the bus.|She was late, she missed the bus|She was late. Because she missed the bus.", "How many clauses are in \"When the bell rang, the students left\"?|Two|One|Three|None"]);
T("Grammar","Active and passive voice","Who does the action?","🎯","",
 ["In the active voice the subject does the action: The girl kicked the ball.", "In the passive voice the subject receives the action: The ball was kicked by the girl.", "The active voice is usually clearer and shorter, so use it most of the time."],
 ["Which sentence is in the active voice?|The chef cooked the meal.|The meal was cooked by the chef.|The meal has been cooked.|The meal was being cooked.", "Which sentence is in the passive voice?|The window was broken by the storm.|The storm broke the window.|The storm is breaking windows.|The storm will break the window.", "Change to active: \"The song was sung by Maya.\"|Maya sang the song.|The song is sung by Maya.|Maya was sung the song.|The song sang Maya.", "Change to passive: \"The dog chased the cat.\"|The cat was chased by the dog.|The cat chased the dog.|The dog was chased by the cat.|The dog is chasing the cat.", "In \"The letter was written by Sam\", who did the action?|Sam|The letter|Nobody|The reader", "Which voice is usually clearer and more direct?|Active voice|Passive voice|Both are the same|Neither", "Which sentence is in the active voice?|Our team won the game.|The game was won by our team.|The game has been won.|The game was being won.", "Which sentence is in the passive voice?|The cake was eaten.|We ate the cake.|She is eating cake.|They will eat cake.", "When is the passive voice useful?|When we do not know who did the action|When we want a longer sentence|When the subject is a person|Never", "Change to active: \"Mistakes were made by the team.\"|The team made mistakes.|Mistakes made the team.|The team was made mistakes.|Mistakes are made."]);
T("Writing","Punctuation for writers","Commas, semicolons, colons and apostrophes","✒️","",
 ["Use a comma after an introductory phrase and before a joining word in a compound sentence.", "A semicolon joins two closely related sentences. A colon introduces a list or an explanation.", "An apostrophe shows ownership (the dog's bowl) or missing letters (don't). Its means belonging to it. It's means it is."],
 ["Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?|I was tired; I went to bed early.|I was; tired and went to bed.|I was tired; and went to bed.|I; was tired.", "Which sentence uses a colon correctly?|Bring three things: a pen, paper and a ruler.|Bring: three things a pen and paper.|Bring three: things.|Bring three things a pen: paper and a ruler.", "Which sentence is correct?|The dog wagged its tail.|The dog wagged it's tail.|The dog wagged its' tail.|The dog wagged its tail's.", "Which sentence is correct?|It's going to rain today.|Its going to rain today.|Its' going to rain today.|It going to rain today.", "Which shows that one girl owns the book?|the girl's book|the girls book|the girls' book|the girl book's", "Which shows that several teachers share a room?|the teachers' room|the teacher's room|the teachers room|the teacher room's", "Where does the comma go? \"After the game we went home.\"|After the word game|After the word After|After the word went|No comma is needed", "Which sentence punctuates speech correctly?|\"Come here,\" said Mom.|\"Come here\", said Mom.|\"Come here\" said, Mom.|Come here, \"said Mom.\"", "Which sentence is correct?|They're bringing their books over there.|Their bringing they're books over there.|There bringing their books over they're.|They're bringing there books over their.", "Which sentence uses commas in a list correctly?|We bought apples, pears and plums.|We bought, apples pears and plums.|We bought apples pears, and, plums.|We, bought apples pears and plums."]);
T("Literature","Literary devices","Simile, metaphor and more","🎭","",
 ["A simile compares two things using like or as. A metaphor says one thing is another.", "Personification gives human qualities to something that is not human. Hyperbole is exaggeration.", "Alliteration repeats the same first sound. Onomatopoeia is a word that sounds like what it means."],
 ["\"Her smile was as bright as the sun.\" What is this?|Simile|Metaphor|Personification|Hyperbole", "\"The classroom was a zoo.\" What is this?|Metaphor|Simile|Alliteration|Onomatopoeia", "\"The wind whispered through the trees.\" What is this?|Personification|Simile|Hyperbole|Alliteration", "\"I have told you a million times.\" What is this?|Hyperbole|Metaphor|Personification|Simile", "\"Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.\" What is this?|Alliteration|Onomatopoeia|Metaphor|Irony", "\"The bees buzzed and the door banged.\" What is this?|Onomatopoeia|Alliteration|Simile|Metaphor", "A fire station burns down. What is this an example of?|Irony|Simile|Alliteration|Rhyme", "What do we call a hint about what will happen later in a story?|Foreshadowing|Flashback|Climax|Setting", "What do we call an object that stands for a bigger idea, like a dove for peace?|Symbol|Simile|Stanza|Plot", "What do we call language that appeals to the five senses?|Imagery|Irony|Dialogue|Theme"]);
T("Literature","Story elements","Plot, conflict, theme and point of view","📖","",
 ["The plot has five parts: exposition, rising action, climax, falling action and resolution.", "The conflict is the main problem. It can be between characters, inside a character, or against nature or society.", "The theme is the message of the story. Point of view is who tells it: first person uses I, third person uses he, she or they."],
 ["What is the turning point or most exciting part of a story called?|Climax|Exposition|Resolution|Setting", "Which part of the plot introduces the characters and setting?|Exposition|Climax|Falling action|Resolution", "Where and when a story takes place is called what?|Setting|Theme|Plot|Conflict", "What is the main character of a story called?|Protagonist|Antagonist|Narrator|Author", "What is the character who works against the main character called?|Antagonist|Protagonist|Narrator|Hero", "A character struggles to decide what is right. What kind of conflict is this?|Character against self|Character against nature|Character against society|Character against character", "A hiker is trapped by a snowstorm. What kind of conflict is this?|Character against nature|Character against self|Character against character|Character against society", "\"I walked into the room and saw my friend.\" Which point of view is this?|First person|Second person|Third person|No point of view", "What is the theme of a story?|Its message about life|The place where it happens|The order of events|The main character's name", "Which part of the plot shows how the problem is solved?|Resolution|Rising action|Exposition|Climax"]);
T("Reading","Reading: The Underground Railroad","Read the passage, then answer","🚂","Answer from the passage.",
 ["Read the whole passage first.", "Look back at the passage to find the answer. Do not guess from memory.", "For questions about meaning, read the sentences around the word."],
 ["What was the Underground Railroad?|A secret network of safe houses and helpers|A train line under the ground|A road built by the government|A ship that crossed the lake", "Where were the travellers going?|Canada|Mexico|England|California", "What were the guides called?|Conductors|Stations|Engineers|Captains", "What were the hiding places called?|Stations|Conductors|Tracks|Tunnels", "Who was Harriet Tubman?|A conductor who had escaped slavery herself|A train driver|A queen|A ship's captain", "About how many people did Harriet Tubman lead north?|About seventy|About seven|About seven hundred|About seven thousand", "Why did they usually travel at night?|It was safer because the journey was dangerous|Nights were warmer|The trains ran at night|They liked the dark", "What did they follow to find their way north?|The North Star|A paper map|The railway tracks|The river only", "In which province did many of them settle?|Ontario|Alberta|Manitoba|Yukon", "What is the main idea of the passage?|Brave people helped others escape to freedom in Canada|Railways were built in the 1800s|Harriet Tubman lived in Ontario|Travelling at night is hard"],"","In the 1800s, thousands of enslaved people escaped from the United States to freedom in Canada. They used a secret network called the Underground Railroad. It was not a real railroad. It was a chain of safe houses and brave helpers. People who guided the travellers were called conductors, and the hiding places were called stations. One famous conductor was Harriet Tubman, who had escaped slavery herself. She went back many times and led about seventy people north. Many of them settled in towns in southern Ontario, such as St. Catharines and Chatham. The journey was dangerous, and they usually travelled at night, following the North Star.");
T("Words","Word choice and tone","Connotation, formal and informal","🗣","",
 ["The denotation of a word is its dictionary meaning. The connotation is the feeling it carries.", "Formal language is for essays and letters to people you do not know. Informal language is for friends.", "Tone is the writer's attitude. Careful word choice makes writing more exact."],
 ["Which word has the most positive connotation?|Slender|Skinny|Scrawny|Bony", "Which word has the most negative connotation?|Stubborn|Determined|Firm|Steady", "Which sentence is the most formal?|I would appreciate your help.|Gimme a hand, will ya?|Help me out here.|Can u help?", "Which is the best opening for a letter to a company?|Dear Sir or Madam,|Hey there,|Yo,|What's up,", "Which word is a more exact choice than \"said\" for speaking very quietly?|Whispered|Shouted|Laughed|Argued", "Which word is a stronger choice than \"walked\" for moving slowly and tiredly?|Trudged|Sprinted|Skipped|Danced", "\"What a wonderful surprise!\" What is the tone?|Joyful|Angry|Bored|Fearful", "\"I suppose we have to go, then.\" What is the tone?|Reluctant|Excited|Proud|Joyful", "What is the dictionary meaning of a word called?|Denotation|Connotation|Tone|Mood", "Which word should you avoid in a formal essay?|Gonna|Therefore|However|Although"]);
grade(8);
T("Grammar","Verbals","Gerunds, participles and infinitives","🏃","",
 ["A gerund is a verb ending in ing that acts as a noun: Swimming is fun.", "A participle is a verb form that acts as an adjective: the broken window, the running water.", "An infinitive is to plus a verb: to read, to play."],
 ["\"Swimming is my favourite sport.\" What is \"swimming\" here?|A gerund|A participle|An infinitive|An adverb", "\"She wants to travel.\" What is \"to travel\"?|An infinitive|A gerund|A participle|A preposition", "\"The barking dog woke us.\" What is \"barking\" here?|A participle|A gerund|An infinitive|A noun", "\"The broken vase lay on the floor.\" What is \"broken\" here?|A participle|A gerund|An infinitive|A conjunction", "\"I enjoy reading.\" What is \"reading\" here?|A gerund|A participle|An infinitive|An adjective", "Which sentence contains an infinitive?|He hopes to win.|He is winning.|Winning feels great.|The winning team cheered.", "Which sentence contains a gerund?|Running keeps me fit.|The running water was cold.|I want to run.|She ran fast.", "Which sentence has a dangling modifier?|Walking to school, the rain started.|Walking to school, I felt the rain start.|I walked to school in the rain.|The rain started as I walked.", "What job does a gerund do in a sentence?|It acts as a noun|It acts as an adjective|It joins two clauses|It shows the tense", "What job does a participle do in a sentence?|It acts as an adjective|It acts as a noun|It acts as a conjunction|It shows ownership"]);
T("Grammar","Agreement and pronouns","Subjects, verbs and pronoun case","🤝","",
 ["A singular subject takes a singular verb. Ignore the words between the subject and the verb.", "Use I, he, she, we and they for subjects. Use me, him, her, us and them for objects.", "A pronoun must agree with the noun it replaces, and it must be clear which noun that is."],
 ["Choose the correct verb: \"The box of pencils ___ on the desk.\"|is|are|were|be", "Choose the correct verb: \"Each of the students ___ a book.\"|has|have|are having|were", "Choose the correct verb: \"Neither the teacher nor the students ___ ready.\"|are|is|was|has", "Choose the correct pronoun: \"Sara and ___ went to the store.\"|I|me|myself|mine", "Choose the correct pronoun: \"The coach gave the trophy to Ali and ___.\"|me|I|myself|we", "Choose the correct word: \"___ is at the door?\"|Who|Whom|Whose|Which one of", "Choose the correct word: \"To ___ did you give the letter?\"|whom|who|whose|which", "Which sentence is correct?|Everyone should bring a lunch.|Everyone should bring they lunch.|Everyone should brings a lunch.|Everyone are bringing lunch.", "Which sentence is correct?|The team is playing well tonight.|The team are play well tonight.|The team be playing well tonight.|The team were play well tonight.", "Which sentence has a clear pronoun?|When Maya met Priya, Maya smiled.|When Maya met Priya, she smiled.|When they met, she smiled at her.|She met her and she smiled."]);
T("Writing","Essay writing","Thesis, paragraphs and transitions","📝","",
 ["An essay has an introduction with a thesis, body paragraphs and a conclusion.", "Each body paragraph has a topic sentence, evidence and an explanation.", "Transition words such as however, therefore and for example link ideas together."],
 ["What is a thesis statement?|The main argument of an essay|The title of an essay|The last sentence of each paragraph|A list of sources", "Where is the thesis statement usually found?|In the introduction|In the second body paragraph|Only in the conclusion|In the title", "What does a topic sentence do?|States the main idea of a paragraph|Ends the essay|Lists the sources|Asks the reader a question", "Which transition shows contrast?|However|Therefore|For example|In addition", "Which transition shows a result?|Therefore|However|Although|For instance", "Which transition adds another point?|Furthermore|In contrast|As a result|Nevertheless", "What should a conclusion do?|Restate the thesis and sum up the main points|Introduce a brand new argument|Repeat the introduction word for word|List only quotations", "What do we call facts, examples and quotations that support a point?|Evidence|Thesis|Transition|Tone", "What is copying someone else's work without giving credit called?|Plagiarism|Paraphrasing|Summarizing|Citing", "What is putting an author's idea into your own words, with credit, called?|Paraphrasing|Plagiarism|Quoting|Editing"]);
T("Writing","Persuasion and media","Ethos, pathos, logos and bias","📣","",
 ["Persuasive writing tries to convince the reader. Ethos appeals to trust, pathos to feelings and logos to reason.", "A fact can be proven. An opinion is what someone thinks or feels.", "Bias is a one-sided view. Check who made a message, why, and what is left out."],
 ["An advertisement shows a famous athlete using a product. Which appeal is this?|Ethos|Logos|Pathos|Irony", "A charity shows a sad, hungry puppy. Which appeal is this?|Pathos|Logos|Ethos|Satire", "\"Studies show this plan cuts costs by 20%.\" Which appeal is this?|Logos|Pathos|Ethos|Hyperbole", "Which of these is a fact?|Ottawa is the capital of Canada.|Ottawa is the best city in Canada.|Everyone loves Ottawa.|Ottawa is too cold.", "Which of these is an opinion?|Hockey is the most exciting sport.|A hockey game has three periods.|A hockey team has six players on the ice.|Hockey is played on ice.", "What is bias?|A one-sided point of view|A list of facts|A kind of graph|A summary", "\"Everybody is buying it, so you should too.\" Which technique is this?|Bandwagon|Testimonial|Statistics|Rhetorical question", "What is a question asked for effect, not for an answer, called?|Rhetorical question|Open question|Thesis|Counter-argument", "What is a counter-argument?|An argument for the other side|The first sentence of an essay|A list of facts|A kind of advertisement", "Which source is usually the most reliable for a school report?|A government or university website|An anonymous social media post|An advertisement|A rumour from a friend"]);
