T("Words","Analogies","Word pairs that match","🔗","Which word finishes the pair?",
 ["An analogy compares two pairs of words that are related in the same way.", "First work out how the first pair is related: hot is the opposite of cold.", "Then pick the word that makes the second pair match."],
 ["Hot is to cold as up is to ___.|down|high|sky|over", "Puppy is to dog as kitten is to ___.|cat|mouse|puppy|fur", "Bird is to nest as bee is to ___.|hive|honey|flower|wing", "Hand is to glove as foot is to ___.|sock|toe|leg|walk", "Author is to book as painter is to ___.|painting|brush|colour|museum", "Day is to night as summer is to ___.|winter|hot|season|July", "Finger is to hand as toe is to ___.|foot|shoe|nail|leg", "Fish is to swim as bird is to ___.|fly|feather|nest|sing", "Small is to tiny as big is to ___.|huge|little|short|wide", "Pencil is to write as scissors are to ___.|cut|paper|sharp|draw", "Ottawa is to Canada as Paris is to ___.|France|Europe|London|French", "Page is to book as key is to ___.|keyboard|lock|door|music"]);
T("Reading","Reading: The tides of Fundy","Read the passage, then answer","🌊","Answer from the passage.",
 ["Read the whole passage first.", "Look back at the passage to find the answer. Do not guess from memory.", "For questions about meaning, read the sentences around the word."],
 ["Where is the Bay of Fundy?|Between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia|Between Ontario and Quebec|Beside British Columbia|In the Arctic", "What is special about the Bay of Fundy?|It has the highest tides in the world|It is the deepest bay in the world|It never freezes|It has no fish", "How often does the sea rush into the bay?|Twice a day|Once a week|Once a day|Every hour", "What shape is the bay?|Long and narrow|Round and wide|Small and square|Short and deep", "Where do the fishing boats sit at low tide?|On the muddy sea floor|In deep water|On the rocks|In a harbour far away", "What are the tall rocks shaped like?|Flowerpots|Whales|Boats|Castles", "What do the rocks become a few hours later?|Small islands|Sand dunes|Bridges|Caves", "How do people travel around the rocks at high tide?|By kayak|On foot|By bicycle|By train", "Why do whales come to the bay each summer?|The tides stir up food|The water is very warm|There are no boats|To see the rocks", "What is the main idea of the passage?|The Bay of Fundy has huge tides that change the shore|Whales are large animals|Kayaks are fun|Fishing is hard work"],"","The Bay of Fundy lies between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia. It has the highest tides in the world. Twice a day, the sea rushes into the long, narrow bay and the water rises. Then it flows back out. At low tide, fishing boats sit on the muddy sea floor, and visitors can walk among tall rocks shaped like flowerpots. A few hours later the same rocks are small islands, and people paddle kayaks around them. The tides also stir up food from the bottom, which brings whales to the bay each summer.");

